WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.560
If you want to hear about how every story
from the original trilogy of Scary Stories

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:07.340
books was used in the movie, then stick around
to the end of this video.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.969
It is the Fall of 1968 in a small town called
Mill Valley, Pennsylvania and Halloween is

00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:18.090
being celebrated in full force.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.320
One thing I noticed was a poster for The Halloween
Horror Double Bill, which presumably was a

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.449
reference to the double feature taking place
at the drive in, where we would later see

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:27.279
the characters watch Night of the Living Dead.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:31.270
But the double bill might also be self-referential,
because at the end of the movie we learn that

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.780
the ending is left open for a possible sequel.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.660
That’s not the only bit of foreshadowing
early on.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.220
One of the main characters, Chuck, wants to
be Spiderman for Halloween, but his mom ends

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.100
up making him a literal spider costume.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.120
His sister, Ruth, bickers with him because
she sees him as a pest, pun intended -- the

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.480
first example of her distaste for arachnids.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.390
Shortly after, when they go to check out the
haunted house, we see that the place is crawling

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:56.680
with spiders -- and at the end of that sequence,
Tommy pushes Ruth into the locked room, where

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.420
she gets spider webs caught in her hair, which
is likely where she first receives the bite

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:03.540
that becomes infected with spider eggs, known
as The Red Spot.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.240
But I’ll discuss that further when we get
to it.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.210
First, let’s talk about the first supernatural
sight, the old lady with the black doberman

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.250
seen by Chuck in the haunted house.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.580
This is likely part of the corrupt Bellows
family that we learn more about throughout

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:20.170
the movie, but the imagery of a black dog
is one that comes up a couple times in Alvin

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.321
Schwartz’s trilogy of books, most notably
the appropriately titled story: The Black

00:01:24.321 --> 00:01:25.880
Dog, from the third book.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.560
In that story, a man named Peter Rothberg
lives alone in an old house.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:36.420
Every night at 11PM, a mysterious black dog
appears and runs down his stairs.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.740
He tries night after night to catch it, but
it's unsuccessful, until one day his neighbor

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.280
sets up downstairs with a pistol.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.950
The dog disappears, never to be seen again,
but he can still hear the dog running on most

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:47.950
nights.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.970
Like the dog in the story, this is the only
time Chuck gets a look at the dog, and naturally,

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:53.790
his friends don’t believe him at first.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:57.570
Stella and Ramón happen across the room that
Sarah Bellows was locked away in, and it is

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:02.250
there that they discover two items, the music
box and Sarah’s book of scary stories.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:06.140
When they find the book, there are a number
of stories already written inside, and they

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.240
correspond to actual stories in from the books
that the movie is sourced from.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.531
The ones I noticed included: May I Carry Your
Basket, a story about a man who offers to

00:02:13.531 --> 00:02:16.700
help an old woman out in the cold one night
with her basket, only to discover that the

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:21.490
basket contains her head, which attacks him;
Somebody Fell From Aloft, the story of a mysterious

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.730
corpse that falls onto a ship at sea; The
Ghost With The Bloody Fingers, the story of

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:30.030
a bloody hand that haunts hotel guests; The
Hook, the story of an escaped convict with

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.170
a hook for a hand, Strangers, a story about
a man who meets a woman reading ghost stories

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:38.500
on the train who proclaims he doesn’t believe
in ghosts only to see the woman vanish before

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.950
him; The Attic, about a guy who steps on a
nail in the attic; Cat’s Paw, about a man

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.610
who shoots the cat who is continually shooting
meat from his smokehouse, only to discover

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:51.090
that it was actually a shapeshifting witch
who was also (plot twist) his neighbor’s

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.710
wife; and Wendigo, the story of a hunter who
goes out on a trip with a Native American

00:02:54.710 --> 00:03:00.180
guide named DeFago, a name I’m sure never
got him made fun of at all during middle school,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.910
who essentially gets dragged away by this
Algonquian mythological creature known as

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:04.910
the Wendigo.

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:08.820
Then there’s a blank page, and on the page
after that Stella sees the beginnings of another

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.989
story, Harold.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:13.980
She smears the red ink with her finger, as
if to suggest that the words had just been

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.880
written and the ink, or as we later find blood,
was freshly.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.240
In the movie, the jock character Tommy lives
on a farm with his mother, and he and his

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.190
friends continually abuse this scarecrow,
Harold, by throwing bottles and hitting it

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:29.260
with a bat, until one night Harold comes to
life and stabs Tommy with a pitchfork, causing

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.170
him to turn into an ugly scarecrow.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.680
The involvement of the character Tommy is
kind of hidden in plain sight in the movie,

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.810
because the story in the book centers around
two farmers, one of which is named Thomas.

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.030
In the book, they create Harold resemble a
farmer they don’t like and similarly abuse

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.380
the scarecrow, but instead of turning Thomas
into a scarecrow, Harold skins him and sets

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:49.830
the skin out to dry in the sun.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.420
The next story to play out in the movie is
the Big Toe.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:56.050
Unlike Harold, the name of the character in
the Big Toe is never stated, but in the movie,

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.650
the name August corresponds with one of Stella’s
two best friends, Augie.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.590
In the book, the boy friends the toe out in
the garden, and so his mom puts it into her

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:07.530
soup, which is ridiculous, so in the movie,
he just finds the stew which appears in the

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.840
fridge and contains the toe and various other
body parts.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.709
The next story is The Red Spot, which is another
one where the name of the character, Ruth,

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:18.049
nicely corresponds with the name of the character
from the original story.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:21.750
In the book, Ruth’s boil bursts to release
the spiders who had nested in her face while

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.949
she’s in the bath, but in the movie, she
rushes into the school bathroom before her

00:04:24.949 --> 00:04:28.620
production of Bye Bye Birdie -- which was
likely chosen because it’s about a rockstar

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.879
named Conrad Birdie who is to perform one
last hit before being drafted into the army,

00:04:32.879 --> 00:04:36.930
much like how the character Ramón’s final
act is helping rescue Stella before he is

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.710
taken overseas by the draft.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.650
The kids try rush to save Ruth from popping
open the red spot, but accidentally run into

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:46.430
the wrong bathroom, where there are these
four girls are just standing in the dark…

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:49.089
wait, what was going on in that first bathroom?

00:04:49.089 --> 00:04:52.689
But anyway, after that, they go to visit Lou
Lou Baptiste, who has a matching music box

00:04:52.689 --> 00:04:56.849
to the one we previously saw in Sarah Bellows’s
room, but this time we get to hear it play,

00:04:56.849 --> 00:04:59.580
and the tune is a song known as “The Hearse
Song.”

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.799
You see, the Scary Stories books are not only
filled with Scary Stories, but also games

00:05:03.799 --> 00:05:07.919
/ pranks to play at Halloween parties or songs
complete with lyrics and sheet music.

00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:12.360
I’m not a musician but I was able to recognize
the melody from the only lyrics that Lou Lou

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.139
mentions about the worms crawling and the
fact that there’s an audiobook version,

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:18.719
where some old guy sings the song.

00:05:18.719 --> 00:05:19.719
Roll it.

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:24.300
“Don’t you ever laugh, as the hearse goes
by.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.229
For you may be the next to die.

00:05:27.229 --> 00:05:31.830
They wrap you up in a big white sheet.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.259
From your head down to your feet.”

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:38.719
Oh no… this song is so scary…

00:05:38.719 --> 00:05:43.889
But it’s around this time that Chuck starts
to mention that he’s been having this recurring

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:47.839
dream, which eventually leads to his own scary
story encounter, where this creature known

00:05:47.839 --> 00:05:52.340
as The Pale Lady corners him from every direction,
and eventually catches him and absorbs him.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.080
The book version is similar but with different
circumstances.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.369
An artist named Lucy Morgan decides to go
out of town to do some painting, but her destination

00:05:59.369 --> 00:06:03.409
changes after a pale lady in a dream tells
her that this is an evil place.

00:06:03.409 --> 00:06:07.110
She ends up going to a town called Dorset,
and when she goes to look at her room, it

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:11.180
is the same as the location from her dream,
just like how Chuck’s dream had described

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.930
the Red Room, which is the room in the hospital
that the kids are looking for.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:19.069
Lucy ends up fleeing the town after seeing
The Pale Lady from her dream, but Chuck isn’t

00:06:19.069 --> 00:06:20.069
so lucky.

00:06:20.069 --> 00:06:23.450
The next one is one that I really did not
expect to see in the movie adaptation, Me

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:27.550
Tie Doughty Walker -- a story from the original
Scary Stories To Tell In The Dark Book.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:31.080
The film version has our characters locked
in a jail cell where this creature called

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.289
The Gangly Man assembles itself after the
dog at the police station watches its body

00:06:35.289 --> 00:06:38.099
parts fall down the chimney, and it goes after
Ramón.

00:06:38.099 --> 00:06:41.130
The book kind of establishes the legend that
Ramón and Stella encounter.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:45.400
It tells of a man who offers $200 to anyone
who stays in this haunted house overnight.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.210
A boy decides to try it and goes there with
his dog.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.339
He goes there and hears someone in the distance
singing the lyrics: “Me tie doughty walker.”

00:06:52.339 --> 00:06:56.889
And then his dog responds by signing “Lynchee
kinchy colly molly dingo dingo.”

00:06:56.889 --> 00:07:00.210
If you listen to the dog’s grunting in the
film, it actually sounds like he is grunting

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:01.210
these lyrics.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:05.270
The singing gets closer until the head falls
down the chimney, and in the book, that’s

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:08.639
where it ends, but the movie really takes
it a step further by having it be the head

00:07:08.639 --> 00:07:11.249
of a creature that continues to come after
Ramón.

00:07:11.249 --> 00:07:14.960
That would seemingly be the last one, but
we eventually come to find out that this entire

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:19.039
thing was part of one big scary story, and
that is The Haunted House.

00:07:19.039 --> 00:07:20.569
Here’s the low-down on The Haunted House.

00:07:20.569 --> 00:07:24.499
It’s the 10th story in book number one,
and it follows a preacher who goes to investigate

00:07:24.499 --> 00:07:27.180
a house said to have been haunted for the
last 10 years.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.879
The ghost of a woman appears and tells the
preacher the story of how her husband killed

00:07:30.879 --> 00:07:33.270
her for her money and buried her in the cellar.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:36.629
She instructs the man to give her a proper
burial, and put her finger in the collection

00:07:36.629 --> 00:07:39.589
plate at church, where it will stick to the
man who is guilty.

00:07:39.589 --> 00:07:42.319
After he’s done this, she’ll tell him
where the money is hidden, and it’s his

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:43.319
to keep.

00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:46.339
This may not sound like anything from the
movie, until you consider the fact that this

00:07:46.339 --> 00:07:48.240
is essentially the WHOLE movie.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.309
These kids wander into a haunted house and
come across the book from Sarah Bellows.

00:07:52.309 --> 00:07:56.050
Little by little, they learn more and more
about her family abused her and killed her

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:59.589
for trying to expose the truth, about how
her family’s paper mill poisoned the water

00:07:59.589 --> 00:08:01.529
supply and got some kids killed.

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:05.240
She’s bringing her killers to justice just
as the woman’s ghost in the haunted house

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:09.490
story did, and instead of publicly exposing
the killer as seen in the book, she gets Stella,

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:13.279
the girl who a few short days ago was too
afraid to write for her school newspaper,

00:08:13.279 --> 00:08:16.309
to write in the book and bring the truth out
about Sarah’s death…

00:08:16.309 --> 00:08:19.610
Even though I’m pretty sure it’s been
70 years and nobody cares anymore.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:22.709
But still, this one small action somehow fixes
everything.

00:08:22.709 --> 00:08:27.169
It’s just like it was told in the beginning
of the movie: stories hurt and stories heal.

00:08:27.169 --> 00:08:30.749
And we roll the credits, which are played
over graphics done in the same style as the

00:08:30.749 --> 00:08:32.390
original artwork from the book trilogy.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:33.390
So that’s it.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:35.530
Scary Stories To Tell In The Dark Number 1.

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:39.020
I recently also did a video on the Top 5 Things
You Missed from It number one.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:42.970
So click the video on the left to see that,
and if you like horror you’re definitely

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:46.990
going to want to subscribe to CZsWorld because
I upload new horrors every week, RING that

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:51.100
deathbell for notifications and I’ll see
you in the next video.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.200
Assuming we both survive.

